### W1: the deploy script's live check and the favicon pair (create; Task; priority Low; labels `deploy-found`, `follow-up`, `rec-2026-11-18`; marker `REC-LND8-18NOV-W1`)
SUMMARY-W1: deploy-package.py live-check lists the favicon route-cache pair a page visit leaves on the web site as SITE-ONLY, so a healthy deploy exits 3 (NOT PROVEN) if anyone opens the site before the check; allow exactly that pair on the web site

BEGIN BODY-W1
BLUF: At main <<MAIN8>>, scripts/deploy-from-ci.sh ends with exit 3 and "NOT PROVEN" (lines 104 to 107) whenever deploy-package.py live-check (line 96) lists any difference, and live-check lists as SITE-ONLY every file on the site that the package does not hold and that is not on its allowed list: the list is SITE_ONLY_ALLOWED (deploy-package.py:75), which holds two Oryx names on the web and none on the API, and it is applied at :541 and :551 to :552. A pair of favicon route-cache files appears on the web site between deploys, .next/server/route-cache/APP_ROUTE/<64 hex>/$/favicon.ico.body (25931 B) and favicon.ico.meta (458 B), which no package holds; the deploy records read it as left by a page visit. So a reviewer who opens the site in the minute between the web deploy and the check turns a healthy deploy into exit 3, DEPLOYED BUT NOT PROVEN. This ticket proposes that live-check name and allow exactly that pair.
Recommendation: Allow exactly that pair, on the web site only, and nothing else. Fix shape (a proposal; the builder decides): in scripts/deploy-package.py add one compiled pattern beside SITE_ONLY_ALLOWED (:75), for the web only, that matches a name of the form .next/server/route-cache/APP_ROUTE/<64 hex>/$/favicon.ico.body or .meta and no other (the API gets none); at :551 leave a name that matches it out of `extra`, so it is not listed at :552 and does not make :592 return 1; print how many were allowed after the existing summary line (:555 to :557), only when the count is above 0, so the lines the existing tests match (infra/tests/test_deploy_hpsmpoc208.py) stay equal. Tests first, beside the two rows at infra/tests/test_deploy_hpsmpoc208.py:385 and :438: the pair on the web is green and counted; the same two names on the API stay red; a sibling stays red (favicon.ico.body.bak, another file under route-cache, a 63-hex folder, a different route name). Then the two places that name the only site-only names accepted (the docstring at deploy-package.py:25 to :30 and infra/README.md:183 to :185). Proposed default if nobody chooses: that change. Alternative: change nothing in the script and read an exit 3 that lists only this pair as healthy; that leaves the next reviewer's visit as a false alarm. This ticket changes nothing and approves nothing.
Detail: Measured by the deploy records of the last three UAT updates (the BEFORE read of each, a download of the web site's wwwroot compared with the previous package): site-only 2, named as the favicon route-cache pair, with site times 2026-11-13T23:38:10Z, 2026-11-16T03:12:46Z and 2026-11-17T17:54:34Z; only the last record prints the full names and sizes. After the update of 2026-11-18 the script's own live check read site-only 0 (the deploy replaces the site with --clean true) and so did a download made before any browser visit, so the pair is created after a deploy and before the next one: 3 of 3 BEFORE reads found it. That a browser visit creates it is the deploy record's reading and is DERIVED, not tested here. The record puts the exposure at about 60 s (21:59:57Z to 22:00:58Z in the last update); the update passed, and whether anyone visited the site in that window is not measurable. Not established: what the 64-hex folder name is derived from, or whether it changes per build; whether any other route can leave a route-cache file; whether the pair's content matters (it cannot be compared, it is not in the package). Searched at filing: the phrase searches for favicon and for route-cache read 0; HPSMPOC-497 (Backlog) is a different defect in the same script (the upload's failure report ends the script before the live check runs); HPSMPOC-215 (Done) is where the Oryx allowance came from (its note N-4); HPSMPOC-208 (Done) is the live check itself.
Done when: live-check on the web site accepts exactly that pair, and no other route-cache file, with a test row that is red without the change; the API stays strict; and the two documents that name the allowed site-only names say so; or the item is recorded as declined.
Records: entry C-<<CE>> in the project's clarifications record.
